Here is a basic algorithm for compressing and decompressing data using the LZ77 method in C++17:
```
#include <iostream>
#include <fstream>
#include <string>

using namespace std;

int main() {
    // Compressing data
    string input;
    ofstream ofs("output.bin");
    cin >> input;
    ofs.write(input.c_str(), input.size());
    ofs.close();

    // Decompressing data
    string decompressed;
    ifstream ifs("output.bin");
    ifs.read((char*)decompressed.c_str(), decompressed.size());
    ifs.close();
    cout << decompressed << endl;

    return 0;
}
```
LZ77 is a lossless data compression algorithm that works by finding repeated sequences, or "matches", in the input data and replacing them with pointers to earlier occurrences of the same data. The compressed data is then stored as a sequence of pairs, each consisting of a match length and the location of the match in the original data.

To decompress the data, the algorithm works by reading the pairs of match length and location from the compressed data, and using them to reconstruct the original data.

This is a very basic implementation of the LZ77 algorithm, and there are many variations and optimizations that can be applied to improve its performance. For example, the algorithm can be optimized to work with a dictionary of previously seen data, rather than relying on a fixed-size window. Additionally, the algorithm can be parallelized to take advantage of multiple cores and improve its compression and decompression speed.

